WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.195
Hi, I'm Neymar O'Danner and this is Module 1 of OS

2
00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:03.940
Work.

3
00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.255
When an AI answer comes out bad, the most common reaction

4
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.720
is to switch tools or write a longer and longer request.

5
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.260
In this module, we'll do it differently.

6
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.795
You'll learn to see AI as a system of pieces, to

7
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.880
choose the model by the task and not by its fame,

8
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.135
to judge an answer with a rubric written in advance, to

9
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.735
find out which account each cost comes from, and to delegate

10
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.095
work with clear limits.

11
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:32.015
We will follow two colleagues from my school, Denise, the academic

12
00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.400
coordinator, and Lucia, who teaches 8th grade science.

13
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.200
Each lesson has a short practice.

14
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:42.215
When I ask, pause the video and do it right then,

15
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.055
with a real task of your own.

16
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.715
When we talk about AI in this course, we mainly mean

17
00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.980
language models, the LLMs.

18
00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:53.335
A model is a mechanism trained to produce text from what

19
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:53.880
you give it.

20
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.595
Notice that last part, from what you give it.

21
00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:01.423
It can't see your school, your team, or last week's meeting.

22
00:01:02.243 --> 00:01:04.698
Whatever is missing from the request, it fills in with the

23
00:01:04.723 --> 00:01:06.763
most common way of answering.

24
00:01:07.323 --> 00:01:08.758
Take Denise's case.

25
00:01:08.783 --> 00:01:11.498
She asked for a plan for the eighth grade parents meeting

26
00:01:11.523 --> 00:01:12.898
and nothing else.

27
00:01:12.923 --> 00:01:16.238
She got back a nice plan with a welcome, the teaching

28
00:01:16.263 --> 00:01:18.298
project, and the test calendar.

29
00:01:18.323 --> 00:01:20.758
None of that was on the school's agenda.

30
00:01:20.783 --> 00:01:23.763
Then she pasted the agenda and the minutes from the previous

31
00:01:23.803 --> 00:01:27.238
meeting and asked it to mark what was still pending.

32
00:01:27.263 --> 00:01:28.378
Same AI.

33
00:01:28.403 --> 00:01:31.978
Now every item pointed to a document she had in hand.

34
00:01:32.003 --> 00:01:35.238
So, if a bad answer isn't always the model's fault, where

35
00:01:35.263 --> 00:01:36.483
does it come from?

36
00:01:36.783 --> 00:01:39.058
This is the central idea of the course.

37
00:01:39.083 --> 00:01:41.138
A model produces answers.

38
00:01:41.163 --> 00:01:44.738
A system organizes how those answers become work.

39
00:01:44.763 --> 00:01:47.578
OS work looks at seven pieces.

40
00:01:47.603 --> 00:01:51.198
The model, the interface where you chat, the files that provide

41
00:01:51.223 --> 00:01:55.643
evidence, the standing instructions, the tools that carry out actions,

42
00:01:55.683 --> 00:01:59.678
the memory between conversations, and the automations that run without

43
00:01:59.703 --> 00:02:00.803
you opening the chat.

44
00:02:02.045 --> 00:02:03.460
Think of a small workshop.

45
00:02:03.485 --> 00:02:08.500
The worker's skill matters, but tools, materials and a quality standard

46
00:02:08.525 --> 00:02:09.840
also decide the result.

47
00:02:09.865 --> 00:02:12.920
When Denise drew the seven boxes for the parents' meeting minutes,

48
00:02:12.945 --> 00:02:15.080
she saw she had a model and an interface.

49
00:02:15.105 --> 00:02:18.640
The files were missing, the agenda was in someone else's email.

50
00:02:18.665 --> 00:02:22.280
And one note, OS work is a metaphor for organization.

51
00:02:22.305 --> 00:02:25.305
You won't be replacing your computer's operating system.

52
00:02:25.745 --> 00:02:28.625
Separating the pieces has a practical benefit.

53
00:02:28.745 --> 00:02:32.525
It stops the reflex of writing a longer and longer request.

54
00:02:32.725 --> 00:02:34.965
First, you ask where the failure started.

55
00:02:35.085 --> 00:02:36.765
Then you fix only that spot.

56
00:02:36.805 --> 00:02:40.280
Lucia's AI said it had saved the grades, but the file

57
00:02:40.305 --> 00:02:41.005
never showed up.

58
00:02:41.885 --> 00:02:44.425
Rewriting the request wouldn't have solved anything.

59
00:02:44.605 --> 00:02:47.665
What was missing was a tool with permission to save.

60
00:02:48.165 --> 00:02:50.780
And there's one piece that never leaves the system.

61
00:02:50.805 --> 00:02:51.465
You.

62
00:02:51.565 --> 00:02:54.500
The model reasons, the tools execute, and you are the one

63
00:02:54.525 --> 00:02:54.960
who checks.

64
00:02:54.985 --> 00:02:56.480
Now, pause the video.

65
00:02:56.505 --> 00:02:59.440
Take a task you've already asked the AI to do, and

66
00:02:59.465 --> 00:03:00.460
draw seven boxes.

67
00:03:00.485 --> 00:03:05.243
Mark, right or wrong for model, interface and files, and put

68
00:03:05.268 --> 00:03:07.703
a question mark on the other four, which come in the

69
00:03:07.728 --> 00:03:08.428
next modules.

70
00:03:09.188 --> 00:03:11.963
Circle the missing piece and write next to it how to

71
00:03:11.988 --> 00:03:12.043
bring it in.

72
00:03:12.068 --> 00:03:15.543
If the model is a piece, what kinds of pieces are

73
00:03:15.568 --> 00:03:15.888
there?

74
00:03:16.148 --> 00:03:18.668
Before comparing names, sort by function.

75
00:03:19.008 --> 00:03:20.048
There are four.

76
00:03:20.308 --> 00:03:24.543
Text models, the LLMs, write, summarize, explain and even code.

77
00:03:24.568 --> 00:03:25.543
Code.

78
00:03:25.568 --> 00:03:28.903
Image models create or edit pictures.

79
00:03:28.928 --> 00:03:31.203
Video models create clips.

80
00:03:31.228 --> 00:03:34.823
And there are classifiers which don't chat.

81
00:03:34.848 --> 00:03:37.583
They receive a set of options and return a choice.

82
00:03:37.608 --> 00:03:39.868
A yes or no or a score.

83
00:03:40.148 --> 00:03:43.403
Lucia listed what she does with AI in a month.

84
00:03:43.428 --> 00:03:46.503
Summarizing the class council minutes, calls for text.

85
00:03:46.528 --> 00:03:49.003
The science fair cover calls for an image.

86
00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:51.308
A 10-second intro calls for video.

87
00:03:51.508 --> 00:03:56.343
and sorting 200 comments from her class into question, praise and

88
00:03:56.368 --> 00:03:58.168
complaint calls for classification.

89
00:03:59.408 --> 00:04:01.603
Each task goes to the kind of model that can do

90
00:04:01.628 --> 00:04:01.888
it.

91
00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:05.720
ChatGPT, Gemini and Copilot are chats.

92
00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:10.375
Behind each one, there are models, organized into families with their

93
00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:10.960
own names.

94
00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:13.415
You don't need to memorize that list.

95
00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:18.475
Names, versions and availability change from one month to the next

96
00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:22.275
and what shows up in a colleague's account may not show

97
00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:23.340
up in yours.

98
00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:26.775
That's why the choice happens when the task comes up, by

99
00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.315
checking the official page and writing down the date.

100
00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:32.835
Denise heard that a certain model was the best and wanted

101
00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:34.400
to use it for everything.

102
00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.600
It worked for the text of the June Festival invitation.

103
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:40.335
When she asked for the invitation artwork, she found out that

104
00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:42.240
model only wrote text.

105
00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:45.975
There are also hubs, like Open Router for text and Key

106
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:47.880
for images and video.

107
00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:51.015
They give you access to many models in one place with

108
00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:52.480
their own credit.

109
00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:56.175
The classifier deserves a closer look, because it's the one people

110
00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:57.055
forget.

111
00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:59.840
It receives a text and a closed list of options.

112
00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:04.151
And it returns a choice, not a paragraph, to sort many

113
00:05:04.176 --> 00:05:06.931
items into a few categories that can be easier to check

114
00:05:06.956 --> 00:05:08.031
than a conversation.

115
00:05:08.056 --> 00:05:11.071
It's a hypothesis to test, not a guarantee.

116
00:05:11.096 --> 00:05:14.531
And you can try it today, right in the chat.

117
00:05:14.556 --> 00:05:18.731
Lúcia pasted a student's comment, I didn't understand the photosynthesis

118
00:05:18.756 --> 00:05:22.751
part that was on the test, along with the three categories,

119
00:05:22.776 --> 00:05:24.851
asking only for the category.

120
00:05:24.876 --> 00:05:26.151
One word came back.

121
00:05:26.176 --> 00:05:27.051
Question.

122
00:05:27.076 --> 00:05:30.091
She checked 10 answers by hand before trusting the rest.

123
00:05:30.116 --> 00:05:31.471
Your practice now.

124
00:05:31.496 --> 00:05:35.931
Pause and write down the three AI tasks you repeat most.

125
00:05:35.956 --> 00:05:38.531
Next to each one, the kind of model.

126
00:05:38.556 --> 00:05:40.731
Only then, a name to test.

127
00:05:40.756 --> 00:05:44.731
Within the same model, there's still a second control.

128
00:05:44.756 --> 00:05:46.271
Reasoning effort.

129
00:05:46.296 --> 00:05:48.811
The model is the mechanism you chose.

130
00:05:48.836 --> 00:05:51.091
Effort is a setting of that mechanism.

131
00:05:51.116 --> 00:05:53.376
How much it analyzes before answering.

132
00:05:53.596 --> 00:05:56.871
Denise thought changing the level meant switching AIs.

133
00:05:56.896 --> 00:05:57.476
It doesn't.

134
00:05:58.156 --> 00:06:00.151
With the same model, you can ask for a quick answer

135
00:06:00.176 --> 00:06:01.976
or a longer analysis.

136
00:06:02.652 --> 00:06:05.652
Higher levels can take more time and use more tokens.

137
00:06:06.232 --> 00:06:09.392
And the names on the selector change from product to product.

138
00:06:09.672 --> 00:06:13.147
In the chat, in work, and in codecs, you'll find different

139
00:06:13.172 --> 00:06:13.472
labels.

140
00:06:14.152 --> 00:06:17.172
There's no single list of levels that applies everywhere.

141
00:06:17.232 --> 00:06:20.987
Some chats don't even show this control, depending on the product

142
00:06:21.012 --> 00:06:22.172
and the plan.

143
00:06:22.232 --> 00:06:25.147
Many people turn on maximum effort for every task, just in

144
00:06:25.172 --> 00:06:25.392
case.

145
00:06:26.172 --> 00:06:28.907
but no amount of effort brings back a document that was

146
00:06:28.932 --> 00:06:30.592
left out of the request.

147
00:06:30.712 --> 00:06:34.067
Lucia wanted to understand why the front office's grade spreadsheet

148
00:06:34.092 --> 00:06:36.092
didn't match hers.

149
00:06:36.952 --> 00:06:40.707
At maximum effort, without pasting the spreadsheets, she got a list

150
00:06:40.732 --> 00:06:43.472
of possible causes, without pointing to any of them.

151
00:06:43.912 --> 00:06:48.067
At the default effort, with both spreadsheets pasted in, she got

152
00:06:48.092 --> 00:06:48.892
the exact role.

153
00:06:50.192 --> 00:06:52.867
One student with a different grade on one assessment.

154
00:06:52.892 --> 00:06:56.847
The right input solved what the control at maximum didn't.

155
00:06:56.872 --> 00:06:58.267
So here's the order.

156
00:06:58.292 --> 00:07:02.523
Start with the default, which handles most everyday tasks.

157
00:07:02.548 --> 00:07:03.983
Complete the inputs.

158
00:07:04.008 --> 00:07:07.523
Only then decide whether the problem needs more analysis, like tasks

159
00:07:07.548 --> 00:07:09.423
with many steps or lots of data.

160
00:07:09.448 --> 00:07:11.963
And how do you know if more effort helped?

161
00:07:11.988 --> 00:07:14.388
By changing just one control.

162
00:07:14.428 --> 00:07:19.003
Repeat the same request, with the same material, in the same

163
00:07:19.028 --> 00:07:21.643
model, and change only the effort.

164
00:07:21.668 --> 00:07:25.348
If you change two things at once, you can't conclude anything.

165
00:07:25.528 --> 00:07:28.483
Then write down whether there was an improvement you can show.

166
00:07:28.508 --> 00:07:33.823
One more fact, one less error, a correct calculation, it got

167
00:07:33.848 --> 00:07:36.763
better, with no evidence, doesn't count.

168
00:07:36.788 --> 00:07:40.143
Lúcia asked for the same annotated correction twice.

169
00:07:40.168 --> 00:07:44.483
At the higher level, a unit error showed up, kilometers instead

170
00:07:44.508 --> 00:07:47.543
of meters, that the default had let slip by.

171
00:07:47.568 --> 00:07:49.283
She wrote down exactly that.

172
00:07:49.308 --> 00:07:51.328
Pause and do the same.

173
00:07:51.568 --> 00:07:55.063
One real request, two rounds in new conversations, and one line

174
00:07:55.088 --> 00:07:58.188
saying whether it improved and what the evidence was.

175
00:07:58.228 --> 00:08:01.323
If both come out the same, that's a result too, the

176
00:08:01.348 --> 00:08:02.408
default is enough.

177
00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:05.080
To say something got better, you need a rubric.

178
00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:10.960
A quality rubric says, before the request, which facts must appear,

179
00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:15.200
which errors are unacceptable, and how the output will be used.

180
00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.080
The key detail is the before.

181
00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:21.440
Written in advance, the rubric doesn't get swayed by the answer.

182
00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:24.780
Written afterward, it tends to approve whatever arrived.

183
00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:27.455
Denise was about to ask for a summary of the science

184
00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:28.735
fair planning meeting.

185
00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:30.860
I want a good summary is a wish.

186
00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:36.815
Any well-written text passes So, before opening the chat, she wrote

187
00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:40.095
three lines on a piece of paper The three people responsible,

188
00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:44.835
with no deadline, that isn't in the minutes A section for

189
00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:48.715
pending items Every sentence can be found in the minutes Three

190
00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:53.395
yes or no questions, answered in under a minute To start,

191
00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:57.595
three criterias are enough Fidelity to the data No data from

192
00:08:57.620 --> 00:09:01.615
the material changes or disappears agreed upon format, the length

193
00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:03.820
and sections you ask for.

194
00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:06.160
Verifiable conclusions.

195
00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:09.100
Nothing is added that can't be found in the material.

196
00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:12.515
These three are the template, and in each task they turn

197
00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:14.000
into concrete items.

198
00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:17.875
In Denise's minutes, fidelity became the three people responsible,

199
00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:19.520
with no invented deadline.

200
00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:22.775
Lucia used the same template for the summary of a chapter

201
00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:25.660
from the science textbook, and the items changed.

202
00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:28.440
Only concepts that are in the chapter.

203
00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:31.755
one page of bullet points and each point with its page

204
00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:32.515
number.

205
00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:36.140
If writing criteria feels like red tape, start with just one.

206
00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:38.680
No data that isn't in the material.

207
00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:41.940
The others show up after the first wrong answer.

208
00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:44.035
Why does this matter so much?

209
00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:47.555
Because without criteria, you pick the prettiest answer.

210
00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:51.220
Denise got two versions of the summary.

211
00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:52.840
The first was smooth.

212
00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:55.815
The meeting was productive and full of energy.

213
00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:59.348
Lucia is organizing the groups Marcos is booking the courtyard by

214
00:09:59.373 --> 00:10:00.388
Friday.

215
00:10:00.413 --> 00:10:02.188
The fair looks promising.

216
00:10:02.213 --> 00:10:03.388
The second was plain.

217
00:10:03.413 --> 00:10:07.813
Three decisions, three people responsible, and one pending item.

218
00:10:08.433 --> 00:10:12.128
With the rubric, the pretty version failed all three items.

219
00:10:12.153 --> 00:10:13.168
Renata was missing.

220
00:10:13.193 --> 00:10:14.728
By Friday wasn't in the minutes.

221
00:10:14.753 --> 00:10:17.328
There were no pending items, and full of energy can't be

222
00:10:17.353 --> 00:10:18.333
found anywhere.

223
00:10:18.533 --> 00:10:20.673
The plain one, passed everything.

224
00:10:21.113 --> 00:10:22.793
Turn the rubric into a table.

225
00:10:22.933 --> 00:10:26.753
Criterion, expected, observed, and passed, yes or no.

226
00:10:27.213 --> 00:10:30.268
And keep the bad answer, because it reminds you what you

227
00:10:30.293 --> 00:10:31.293
want to avoid.

228
00:10:31.613 --> 00:10:34.388
Pause and write your three-line rubric for the next summary you're

229
00:10:34.413 --> 00:10:35.633
going to ask for.

230
00:10:35.833 --> 00:10:38.108
Testing two models costs something.

231
00:10:38.133 --> 00:10:42.048
And the question is, which account does that cost come from?

232
00:10:42.073 --> 00:10:44.268
A test may be drawing on a different account than you

233
00:10:44.293 --> 00:10:45.013
think.

234
00:10:45.293 --> 00:10:48.788
The first path is signing in with your account by logging

235
00:10:48.813 --> 00:10:50.008
in to the chat.

236
00:10:50.033 --> 00:10:53.568
Then you use that account's rights and limits, which come from

237
00:10:53.593 --> 00:10:54.953
the plan and the workspace.

238
00:10:56.153 --> 00:10:58.613
Lúcia uses the chat through the school's account.

239
00:10:58.693 --> 00:11:01.508
When she hit the daily usage limit, she found out the

240
00:11:01.533 --> 00:11:03.388
limit belonged to the school's plan, not to her.

241
00:11:03.413 --> 00:11:05.873
And those limits change.

242
00:11:05.953 --> 00:11:09.428
The source of truth is your account's current settings, not what

243
00:11:09.453 --> 00:11:10.173
someone told you.

244
00:11:11.193 --> 00:11:13.933
The second path is the API key.

245
00:11:14.013 --> 00:11:16.928
Programs use AI through an API with a key built by

246
00:11:16.953 --> 00:11:18.773
usage on the platform.

247
00:11:18.893 --> 00:11:21.908
Subscribing to the chat doesn't give you a free balance for

248
00:11:21.933 --> 00:11:23.853
any program that calls the API.

249
00:11:25.133 --> 00:11:28.368
There are two accounts with two builds, even when the model

250
00:11:28.393 --> 00:11:29.213
has a similar name.

251
00:11:29.633 --> 00:11:32.948
Denise ran a reporting program with a school key and the

252
00:11:32.973 --> 00:11:36.468
tokens used showed up on the API platform, not on the

253
00:11:36.493 --> 00:11:36.948
subscription.

254
00:11:36.973 --> 00:11:43.808
The third path is hubs, like OpenRouter and Key, with their

255
00:11:43.833 --> 00:11:44.828
own credit.

256
00:11:44.853 --> 00:11:48.288
Lucia made the Reading Week posters in a hub and the

257
00:11:48.313 --> 00:11:50.253
credit ran out in the middle of the batch.

258
00:11:50.433 --> 00:11:53.708
The chat subscription was still active, but it didn't cover that.

259
00:11:53.733 --> 00:11:56.868
The fix is to top up the credit in the hub

260
00:11:56.893 --> 00:11:57.495
itself.

261
00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:02.555
Before leaving something running for a long time, check which method

262
00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:03.560
is active.

263
00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:06.475
In the tool settings, look at the account plan or the

264
00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:07.360
key news.

265
00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:12.075
In module 3, in Codex, a terminal command, CodexLoginStatus, will

266
00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:13.055
show you this.

267
00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:16.715
And write the method on the project sheet, account, key, or

268
00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:17.440
hub.

269
00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:22.015
The credential itself never goes on the sheet, into a request,

270
00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:22.720
or into a screenshot.

271
00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:25.855
Before asking for the summary of the year's 40 sets of

272
00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:31.275
minutes, Denise wrote, School chat, school account, plan limit, no

273
00:12:31.300 --> 00:12:34.755
password at all, pause and make your own sheet, with one

274
00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:37.175
line for each AI tool you use.

275
00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:39.815
If today you only use the chat with your own account,

276
00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:41.155
the sheet has one line.

277
00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:41.700
And that's fine.

278
00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:46.495
If you find a key pasted into some document, delete it

279
00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:49.315
from there and let whoever manages the account know.

280
00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:51.755
With the right account, how far should you let the AI

281
00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:52.600
go on its own?

282
00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:56.355
In a chat, you send a message, get an answer, and

283
00:12:56.380 --> 00:12:58.055
see each step before the next one.

284
00:12:58.080 --> 00:12:59.515
An agent is different.

285
00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:03.007
It gets a goal and keeps going on its own.

286
00:13:03.032 --> 00:13:08.827
It reads files, creates, compares, fixes, that saves work, and it

287
00:13:08.852 --> 00:13:12.167
also multiplies the points where it can make mistakes without you

288
00:13:12.192 --> 00:13:13.327
seeing.

289
00:13:13.352 --> 00:13:16.547
Lucia asked an agent to organize the quarters grades.

290
00:13:16.572 --> 00:13:20.787
It read three spreadsheets, created a new one, and renamed the

291
00:13:20.812 --> 00:13:21.647
old ones.

292
00:13:21.672 --> 00:13:24.147
She hadn't asked for that last step.

293
00:13:24.172 --> 00:13:27.967
Without a written limit, an agent can burn time, credit, or

294
00:13:27.992 --> 00:13:29.367
touch the wrong file.

295
00:13:29.392 --> 00:13:34.087
Autonomy, here, is an authorization with scope, not an invitation

296
00:13:34.112 --> 00:13:35.747
to do anything at all.

297
00:13:35.772 --> 00:13:37.987
An authorization has five parts.

298
00:13:38.012 --> 00:13:42.547
The goal, the allowed files, the authorized actions, the time limit,

299
00:13:42.572 --> 00:13:43.767
and the stop point.

300
00:13:43.792 --> 00:13:47.047
Denise wrote one for the agent that prepares the monthly attendance

301
00:13:47.072 --> 00:13:48.827
report.

302
00:13:48.852 --> 00:13:51.052
Goal, a draft of the September report.

303
00:13:52.592 --> 00:13:55.747
only the September attendance spreadsheet.

304
00:13:55.772 --> 00:14:01.387
read and create the draft without deleting or renaming anything.

305
00:14:01.412 --> 00:14:04.787
time up to 10 minutes.

306
00:14:04.812 --> 00:14:05.507
stop.

307
00:14:05.532 --> 00:14:08.679
deliver the draft and stop before sending.

308
00:14:08.704 --> 00:14:11.839
It took two minutes and fits on a sticky note.

309
00:14:11.864 --> 00:14:12.899
And note.

310
00:14:12.924 --> 00:14:16.739
Sending, publishing and deleting are external actions.

311
00:14:16.764 --> 00:14:19.579
Each one requires an explicit instruction.

312
00:14:19.604 --> 00:14:22.979
The fact that a send button exists doesn't mean the agent

313
00:14:23.004 --> 00:14:24.759
can use it.

314
00:14:24.784 --> 00:14:26.539
The tool limits what's possible.

315
00:14:26.564 --> 00:14:28.584
The authorization says what's wanted.

316
00:14:29.604 --> 00:14:33.219
To wrap up, always ask for a three-part output.

317
00:14:33.244 --> 00:14:36.819
What was done, how it was verified, and what's still pending.

318
00:14:36.844 --> 00:14:40.439
That way, the result becomes something you review, not something you

319
00:14:40.464 --> 00:14:42.699
have to take on faith.

320
00:14:42.724 --> 00:14:44.744
Denise's agent answered like this.

321
00:14:45.424 --> 00:14:46.139
Done.

322
00:14:46.164 --> 00:14:49.399
The draft with attendance by class.

323
00:14:49.424 --> 00:14:50.359
Verified.

324
00:14:50.384 --> 00:14:53.319
Each class total matches the sum of its roles.

325
00:14:53.344 --> 00:14:53.879
Pending.

326
00:14:53.904 --> 00:14:58.059
8th grade class C has two rows with no date, which

327
00:14:58.084 --> 00:14:59.359
it didn't fill in.

328
00:14:59.384 --> 00:15:02.344
Notice that the pending item wasn't filled with a guess.

329
00:15:02.484 --> 00:15:04.859
Denise checked one of the numbers in the spreadsheet and approved

330
00:15:04.884 --> 00:15:05.419
the rest.

331
00:15:05.444 --> 00:15:06.464
Your practice.

332
00:15:07.584 --> 00:15:11.731
Pause and write a five-part authorization for a small task with

333
00:15:11.756 --> 00:15:14.811
fictional data and send it in the chat.

334
00:15:14.836 --> 00:15:17.971
Read the closing and check one of the points yourself, straight

335
00:15:17.996 --> 00:15:19.791
against the material.

336
00:15:19.816 --> 00:15:21.131
Let's put it all together.

337
00:15:21.156 --> 00:15:23.611
When AI gets it wrong, you look for the missing piece

338
00:15:23.636 --> 00:15:25.371
before rewriting the request.

339
00:15:25.396 --> 00:15:29.731
You choose the kind of model by function before the name,

340
00:15:29.756 --> 00:15:32.611
you test one control at a time and only accept an

341
00:15:32.636 --> 00:15:33.891
improvement with evidence.

342
00:15:33.916 --> 00:15:37.631
You write the rubric before asking and decide by the rubric,

343
00:15:37.656 --> 00:15:38.996
not by the tone.

344
00:15:39.176 --> 00:15:41.571
You know which account each cost comes from.

345
00:15:41.596 --> 00:15:44.471
And you write down the method, never the credential.

346
00:15:44.496 --> 00:15:49.571
And you delegate with scope, a stop point and a closing

347
00:15:49.596 --> 00:15:53.596
that says what was done, verified and pending.

348
00:15:53.776 --> 00:15:57.031
When you have about 30 minutes, do the module lab in

349
00:15:57.056 --> 00:15:59.151
the supplementary material for lesson 6.

350
00:15:59.176 --> 00:16:02.131
The same request in two modules, judged by your rubric.

351
00:16:02.156 --> 00:16:06.596
In the next module, we'll look at chat, work and desktop.

352
00:16:06.876 --> 00:16:10.911
Three ways to work with AI and when to use each

353
00:16:10.936 --> 00:16:11.611
one.

354
00:16:11.636 --> 00:16:12.663
See you there.
